From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:38:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: Add a cache that fetches and keeps media resources for
 players

Nothing caches media in Servo apart from the backends' own buffers:
every loop, seek back, replay, second element and reload downloads the
resource again, and the media element manages each player's fetch, with
its own backoff, suspension and restarts.

Add the crate servo-media-cache, a process-wide cache that fetches
media resources on behalf of their readers and keeps them in blocks of
64 KiB, as Gecko's MediaCache and Chromium's MultiBuffer do. A resource
is identified by its URL, CORS mode and the origin of the document, is
shared by every reader that opens it, and is reused for 300 s after its
last reader is gone.

Storage: a resource of known length up to 8 MiB keeps its blocks in
memory, all of them together within 32 MiB, beyond which unused ones go
first and then new ones use the file. Every other resource uses slots of
one anonymous temporary file in the directory of the pref
media_cache_directory, or the system temporary directory, so that its
data is page cache that the kernel can reclaim. If the file cannot be
created, read or written, blocks stay in memory within 32 MiB. When the
budget is used up, the block whose next use is predicted latest goes
first, as with Gecko's PredictNextUse; the read-ahead of the readers is
protected, up to half of the budget, and blocks read for metadata go by
their last use.

Fetching: a resource has at most two fetches, started through a Fetcher
that the media element implements, for the readers that still need
them. The main fetch loads 30 s ahead of each reader at its rate, else
8 MiB, is suspended there and resumes below 10 s, else 2 MiB. A reader
that only needs metadata loads 1 MiB ahead, and an idle one nothing. A
read more than 1 MiB from every fetch starts a second fetch at its
block, so that the probe of an index at the end of a file costs one
small request; the last few read positions of a reader keep their
fetches, but only the current one starts fetches. A fetch that reaches
cached data stops unless the next missing byte is within 1 MiB. A
declined suspension cancels a fetch with range support, which restarts
at the first missing byte once it is needed, and so does a suspension
that lasts 30 s; without range support, the fetch keeps loading within
its share of the protected read-ahead, since a restart fetches the
resource from its start again and discards what lies before the read.
Requests after the first carry If-Range. Each response is verified as
HTML's "verify a media response" asks: a CORS-cross-origin response from
another origin than the earlier ones fails the load, and any
CORS-cross-origin response taints the media data. A 200 to a range
request with the same validator and length makes the resource
sequential; a differing validator or length fails the readers with
ResourceChanged instead of splicing two versions of the resource. A
fetch that ends early is restarted where it stopped while it stores new
data, and otherwise the readers fail with a network error; a 416 ends
the resource.

Reading: readers never block. A read returns the cached bytes at a
position, or Pending, and the reader's waker is called once data is
there. An observer hears of the metadata, progress at most every 350
ms, suspension, resumption and completion of the download, and of
errors. The cache queues its calls of fetchers, wakers and observers,
and the user objects it drops, under its lock, and one thread at a time
makes them once the lock is released; file I/O also happens without the
lock, on pinned slots. Time comes from a clock that tests replace. A reader that a player still holds can
be closed, which detaches it as dropping it does and fails its later
reads; a reader also tells whether the resource is tainted, before its
metadata event arrives.

New prefs set the directory, budgets and thresholds:
media_cache_directory, media_cache_file_budget_mb (128 on OpenHarmony
and Android, 512 elsewhere), media_cache_memory_resource_limit_kb,
media_cache_memory_fallback_mb, media_cache_readahead_seconds,
media_cache_resume_seconds, media_cache_suspend_seconds and
media_cache_reuse_seconds. `./mach test-unit -p servo-media-cache` runs
the tests of the crate. Players may read a reader directly through the
new Player::set_data_source, which backends that cannot refuse with
PlayerError::NotSupported.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: 54 unit tests with a fake fetcher and a fake clock, which
cover sharing, suspension and resumption, gap and probe fetches,
resources without range support or of unknown length, validators and
200s to range requests, origin verification and taint, recovery from
early ends, 416, eviction, memory and file backing and their I/O
errors, the reuse window, trimming, activities, wakers and observers
called without the lock and dropped from inside callbacks, and readers
on other threads, and closing a reader that a player holds.

Validation: `./mach test-unit -p servo-media-cache` passes ten runs in a
row; each of 22 deliberate breaks makes at least one test fail. Two
independent reviews of the crate; their findings are fixed. `./mach
check`, `cargo clippy -p servo-media-cache --tests` and `./mach fmt`
pass.

AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implemented, reviewed, tested and mutation-checked by agents)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
